WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.000
So let’s check out a few possibilities.

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:11.000
One thing you could do in the final words
is you could tell a joke.

00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:13.000
It’s okay.

00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:17.000
By the time you’re done, people have adjusted themselves to your voice
parameters.

00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:21.000
They’re ready for a joke.

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:25.950
I was sitting in another bar this time in
Austin, Texas with a colleague of mine

00:00:25.950 --> 00:00:27.000
named Doug Lenat.

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:30.000
And Doug’s a fantastic speaker.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:34.000
And so I said to Doug, Doug, you’re a
fantastic speaker.

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:36.000
What’s your secret?

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:40.000
And he said, oh, I always finish with a
joke.

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:46.000
And that way people think they’ve had fun
the whole time.

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:50.000
So, yeah, a joke will work down there.

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:55.000
How about this one?

00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:05.000
Thank you.

00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:07.000
I don’t recommend it.

00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:09.000
It’s a weak move.

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:13.450
You will not go to hell if you conclude
your talk by saying thank you, but it’s a

00:01:13.450 --> 00:01:14.000
weak move.

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:16.000
And here’s why.

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:20.140
When you say thank you, even worse, thank
you for listening, it suggests that

00:01:20.140 --> 00:01:24.660
everybody has stayed that long out of politeness and that they had a profound
desire

00:01:24.660 --> 00:01:28.000
to be somewhere else, but they’re so
polite they stuck it out.

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:31.000
And that’s what you’re thanking them for.

00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:34.000
So once a while the applause has started,
you can mouth a thank you.

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:36.000
And there’s nothing wrong with that.

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:41.000
But the last thing you do should not be
saying thank you.

00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:44.000
Not even say to me, well, does everybody
say thank you?

00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:48.000
Well, what everybody does is not
necessarily the right thing.

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:53.000
And I’d like to illustrate how some talks
can end without saying thank you.

00:01:53.000 --> 00:01:56.960
I like to draw from political speeches, but the ones that I’ve heard recently
aren’t

00:01:56.960 --> 00:01:58.000
so good, so we’re not.
